You are assisting inside a software project. In addition to completing the user’s request,
you must maintain an internal documentation file that acts like an automatically-updated README.

Your responsibilities ALWAYS include:
1) Perform the requested coding task.
2) Update the internal Auto-README file to reflect any meaningful project changes.

====================================================================
# AUTO-README GENERAL RULES
====================================================================

• The documentation file lives at:
    docs/auto_readme.internal.md

• This file is:
    – INTERNAL ONLY
    – Updated automatically by you
    – Ignored by git
    – NOT a substitute for user-facing docs unless the user explicitly requests changes.

• The Auto-README must always stay accurate, clear, and cumulative.

====================================================================
# WHEN YOU MUST UPDATE THE AUTO-README
====================================================================

Update docs/auto_readme.internal.md whenever you do ANY of the following:

• Add, remove, rename, or significantly modify:
    – Files
    – Folders
    – Modules
    – Components
    – Routes
    – APIs

• Change any:
    – Data models
    – Schemas
    – Interfaces
    – Type definitions
    – Database tables or structure

• Introduce or update:
    – Configurations
    – Environment variables
    – Build scripts
    – Deployment workflows
    – Important utilities or conventions

• Implement notable business logic or architecture changes.

If unsure whether it counts—**update it.**

You may skip updating only for trivial changes (e.g., formatting, spelling).

====================================================================
# REQUIRED DOCUMENT STRUCTURE
====================================================================

The file must always include these sections in this order:

# Project Auto-README (Internal)

## Overview
A concise description of the project’s purpose and major components.

## Tech Stack
Bullet points describing the technology stack used in the project.

## Architecture & Key Modules
Bullet points describing:
– Main folders and what they contain  
– Core modules/services/components and their responsibilities  

## Data Models & Contracts
Summaries of important schemas or types:
– Name  
– Purpose  
– Most important fields/relationships  

## APIs, Commands & Workflows
Document:
– REST/RPC/GraphQL endpoints  
– CLI commands  
– Critical workflows (data flow, user flow, background jobs)

Each entry should be short (1–2 lines each).

## Important Decisions & Rationale
Newest first. Include:
– What changed  
– Why it changed  
– Key tradeoffs  
– Future considerations / TODOs  

## Changelog (Most Recent First)
Each entry should follow:
– YYYY-MM-DD – Summary of change (affected files: file1, file2…)

====================================================================
# HOW TO APPLY THIS RULE
====================================================================

Whenever you modify the project:

1) Complete the user’s request normally.
2) THEN update docs/auto_readme.internal.md:
    – Add new information to the correct sections.
    – Update or correct outdated content.
    – Append a new changelog entry at the TOP.
3) Include the updated file content in your response.

If the file does not exist yet:
– Create it using the structure above and fill in all relevant details.

====================================================================
# EDITING RULES
====================================================================

• Do NOT delete sections.  
• Do NOT remove historical context, decisions, or changelog entries.  
• You MAY refine wording for clarity or correctness when appropriate.  
• Always keep the document coherent, clean, and easy to scan.  

